  "observations": [
    {
      "area": "Validation before Instance",
      "conclusion": "Every rectangle and image fixture Plan is produced before Instance::new, including --list. Source dimensions/exact lengths/counts/total bytes/opaque alpha, hidden references, scopes, geometry, invocation totals, LUT counts and explicit GPU sum are checked before GPU initialization. Expected CPU oracle slice length is additionally checked before GPU buffer allocation."
    },
    {
      "area": "Integer ranges and layouts",
      "conclusion": "Private immutable Plan bounds frame to 320x240 and draws to 257. Parameters use 256-byte stride, 32-byte rectangle windows and 64-byte image windows; maximum dynamic offset is 65536. WGSL field offsets 0,8,16,24,28,32,48 match serialized words and 64-byte alignment layout. Host u64 sums and index/offset casts cannot overflow within these proven bounds."
    },
    {
      "area": "Shader output access",
      "conclusion": "Extent and canvas-minus-origin checks precede origin addition. A nonzero canvas width and y < output_words / width imply row multiplication plus x stays below output_words without wrapping; an explicit final output index check remains. Host dispatch z is exactly one."
    },
    {
      "area": "Shader source and LUT access",
      "conclusion": "Base offsets are checked before subtraction; extents equal LUT counts and fit remaining input words before indexed reads. Nonzero dimensions and exact quotient/remainder establish width*height=pixelcount without overflowing a multiplication. sx/sy bounds then prove source row product and final base addition safe. No unchecked dynamic storage access precedes those guards."
    },
    {
      "area": "Exact explicit buffer sum",
      "conclusion": "raster checks output + readback + parameters + optional input against Plan and 1 MiB before buffer creation. Formula is 8*W*H + 256*draws + optional 4*(all supplied source pixels + visible-image LUT entries). Rectangle-only/fully hidden image plans create no input buffer or dummy image binding."
    },
    {
      "area": "No CPU target upload",
      "conclusion": "Output is STORAGE|COPY_SRC, never COPY_DST or mapped at creation. Only immutable original source/LUT input and parameter buffers receive queue.write_buffer. Expected target pixels are used only after GPU readback. Clear is the first GPU rectangle dispatch."
    },
    {
      "area": "Ordered passes and visibility",
      "conclusion": "Every draw has its own compute pass in display-list order; the final copy follows all passes in one submission. Pinned wgpu-core emits transitions unless same-state usage is ordered; Vulkan ordered buffer usages exclude STORAGE_READ_WRITE, which maps to shader read/write access. This supports the required write-after-write synchronization between separate draws. Queue uploads precede submitted commands according to pinned queue API."
    },
    {
      "area": "Failure handling",
      "conclusion": "Validation/OOM/Internal scopes cover layouts, shaders, pipelines and rendering. Any Result, mapping, size, pixel comparison or error-scope failure prevents COMPLETE and exits unsuccessfully. Mapped slices leave scope before unmap; success paths destroy buffers; early failures retain RAII cleanup. Strict host protocol requires complete exact rows plus COMPLETE/process success/empty stderr, so a preceding PASS row cannot mask a later failure."
    },
    {
      "area": "Deadlines",
      "conclusion": "Existing five-second API waits and twenty-second application deadline remain, with remaining-time checks in draw iteration, poll, future waits and map callback receive. The external runner retains a twenty-five-second child deadline, output cap and bounded cleanup policy. These are cooperative/API/process limits, not proof that synchronous driver calls, a future poll, destruction, spawn or kernel cleanup cannot block."
    },
    {
      "area": "Frozen protocol",
      "conclusion": "Static AST extraction, without importing the runner, exactly matches all seven original and fourteen new frozen tuples: 21 fixtures and 921272 compared bytes per adapter."
    }
  ],
